From 3779990788cbe5427ad46a26e5f229cc4f4877cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Na Song <nasong@qti.qualcomm.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 15:36:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix: stop leaking DSP mappings when a signal arrives
 mid-inference

The HTP backend reaches the DSP through a blocking fastrpc ioctl. When
SIGINT is delivered to the thread sitting in that ioctl it returns EINTR,
QNN reports "DspTransport call failed, error 0x0000000f" and the DSP side
session is left in an error state. Every unmap and contextFree afterwards
then fails, and the mappings survive process exit:

  <E> fastrpc memory failed to unmap for fd: 43 with length: 58720256
      failed with error: 0x14
  <E> Failed to free context with err 1003

That is ~60 MiB per launch cycle for a Depth-Anything-V2 sized model
(56 MiB weights plus input, output and metadata buffers). After 65
start/stop cycles no PD has room left and the model can no longer be
loaded at all:

  <E> Failed to find available PD for contextId 1 on deviceId 0 coreId 0
      with context size estimate 183453952

Only a reboot recovers the board. The process still reports a clean exit
throughout, so the leak is silent.

- Block SIGINT and SIGTERM for the duration of inference_execute() via an
  RAII guard. The signals stay pending, the ioctl is not interrupted and
  the DSP session stays healthy, at the cost of one inference period of
  extra shutdown latency.
- Serialize inference_execute() against ~QnnInference() with a mutex, so
  the context is never freed while the DSP is still executing a graph,
  and reject requests that arrive once teardown has started.
- Give QrbRosInferenceNode a destructor that drops the subscription
  before the inference manager, so no callback can be dispatched into a
  half-destroyed manager.

A downstream node cannot work around this: ros2 launch forwards SIGINT to
every process in the same instant, so draining in-flight requests on the
client side has no way to delay this node's teardown.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Signed-off-by: Na Song <nasong@qti.qualcomm.com>
